  "question": {
    "text": "<p>The electric field at a point associated with a light wave is given by<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>sin</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Vm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Given:&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eVs</mi></math></p><p>If this light falls on a metal surface having a work function of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>eV</mi></math>, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons will be</p>"
